---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import lidarDem from '../../assets/lidarDEM.png';
import lidarSetup from '../../assets/charterstowerlidarsetup.jpg';
import accessTracks from '../../assets/accesstrackscenary.jpg';
---
<Base
  title="Drone LiDAR Surveys WA & Australia | DEMs, Contours, Point Clouds | Magtech Surveys"
  description="Drone LiDAR surveys for exploration logistics, earthworks, vegetation and terrain mapping across Australia. Ground-classified point clouds, bare-earth DEMs, DSMs and contours with RTK base station and ground control positioning."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">SERVICES / LIDAR</p>
      <span class="ramp-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <h1>LiDAR surveys</h1>
      <p class="lede">Survey-grade terrain and canopy mapping, from single sites to multi-tenement programs.</p>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          LiDAR measures the ground directly, with enough pulses finding gaps in the canopy to
          map the surface beneath vegetation. That makes it the right tool where photogrammetry
          struggles: under canopy, over low-texture surfaces, and anywhere a true bare-earth
          terrain model matters. We fly it for exploration access and
          logistics planning, drill pad and earthworks design, drainage and hydrology, and
          vegetation structure mapping.
        </p>
        <p>
          Positioning is controlled with an on-site RTK base station and surveyed ground
          control points, with CORS post-processing where site conditions call for it, giving
          reliable absolute accuracy even at remote sites with no established survey control.
          Point clouds are ground-classified and delivered with the derived products your
          engineers or geologists actually use.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="media">
        <Image src={lidarDem} alt="Bare-earth digital elevation model hillshade from a drone LiDAR survey, showing fine terrain and drainage detail" />
        <figcaption>Bare-earth DEM hillshade from a drone LiDAR survey. Ground surface and drainage resolved beneath vegetation.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="media">
        <Image src={accessTracks} width={1300} alt="Remote exploration access tracks through scrub, mapped for logistics planning" />
        <figcaption>Terrain and access mapping supports exploration logistics in remote country.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <table class="spec">
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Typical accuracy</th><td>~5 cm absolute, 2&ndash;3 cm relative (surface and conditions dependent)</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Point density</th><td>Matched to purpose; we do not sell density beyond what improves accuracy</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Positioning</th><td>On-site RTK base station and ground control points; CORS post-processing where appropriate</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Deliverables</th><td>Classified LAS/LAZ point cloud, bare-earth DEM, DSM, contours, hillshade. GeoTIFF and CAD-ready formats. GDA2020.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <figure class="media">
        <Image src={lidarSetup} width={1300} alt="Drone and LiDAR payload set up beside the survey vehicle in the field" />
        <figcaption>LiDAR payload and platform, field setup.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>LiDAR or photogrammetry?</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          They overlap, and we quote whichever suits the job rather than the one with the higher
          price. As a rule: vegetation, bare-earth terrain models or uniform surfaces like mown
          turf point to LiDAR. Visual records, orthomosaics and stockpile volumetrics on
          well-textured ground point to photogrammetry. If you are not sure, describe the end
          product you need and we will recommend the method, including telling you if the cheaper
          one is enough.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Cta />
</Base>
